<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josef Terboven</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Josef Terboven was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> official and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a> whom Hitler appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichskommissar">Reich Commissioner</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in April 1940. He held real power over the occupied country from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>, working with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidkun-quisling">Vidkun Quisling</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasjonal-samling">Nasjonal Samling</a> while repressing resistance through arrests and executions. He killed himself in May 1945 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> surrendered.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Josef Terboven was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a> on 23 May 1898, the son of minor landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> descent. He volunteered for military service in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">field artillery</a> and the new air force, won the Iron Cross and was discharged as a lieutenant in December 1918. He studied law and political science at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and Freiburg but left in 1922 without a degree, and worked as a bank clerk until June 1925. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> in November 1923 and took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From August 1925 Terboven worked full time for the party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>, founding its local group and editing the weekly The New Front. Named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a> of Essen on 1 August 1930, he kept the post through the Nazi period. He sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> from September 1930, became a member of the Prussian State Council in 1933, and in 1934 married Ilse Stahl, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a>'s former secretary; Hitler witnessed the wedding. In 1935 he became Oberpräsident of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a>, uniting party and state offices in his area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Terboven was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichskommissar">Reichskommissar</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> on 24 April 1940, before the invasion was complete, and answered only to Hitler. He saw himself as a viceroy with limitless power of command. He had authority over the small German civilian administration, not over the 400,000 army troops under Nikolaus von Falkenhorst, but he commanded a force of around 6,000 men. On 25 September 1940 he dismissed Norway's Administrative Council, appointed a Provisional State Council, declared King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-vii">Haakon VII</a> deposed and banned every party except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidkun-quisling">Vidkun Quisling</a>'s Nasjonal Samling. A Quisling government followed in February 1942.</p><p>He set up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>, among them Falstad and Bredtvet, and ordered the Beisfjord massacre of 18 July 1942, in which about 288 Yugoslav prisoners were shot or burned to death. SS and Police Tribunals sentenced some 150 Norwegians to death. After two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> men were killed at Telavåg in 1942, he led a reprisal that burned the village; 72 were deported, and 31 of them died in captivity. About 770 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In October 1944 a scorched earth policy in Finnmark forced 50,000 people to leave. Dönitz dismissed him on 7 May 1945, and on 8 May he killed himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dynamite">dynamite</a> in a bunker at Skaugum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Josef Terboven” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King Haakon VII and the government refused to surrender and went into exile in London, and in April 1940 Hitler appointed <b>Josef Terboven</b> as Reich Commissioner.</blockquote><a href="/state/reichskommissariat-norwegen">Reichskommissariat Norwegen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Skaugum, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.45,7.01&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Essen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Josef Terboven"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5373 5709h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5373 5709h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.45,7.01&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
